---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="Security"
  description="How ServerPerch is built: a self-hosted appliance, mutually authenticated agent connections, signed licences and releases, and support bundles that are pseudonymised and encrypted before they leave your estate."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Security</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        ServerPerch is a patch-management platform — it sits in the middle of your
        estate, so it is engineered as if it were the most sensitive host on your
        network. This page summarises the model; the full operator security
        documentation ships with the product.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Self-hosted, no outbound dependency</h2>
      <p>
        The platform runs on a virtual appliance inside your network. A running
        appliance does not depend on serverperch.com being reachable: licences are
        <strong>signed documents verified locally</strong>, and a daily licence check-in
        renews a lease with a grace window rather than gating operations. Missed
        check-ins never stop agents reporting in or block you during an incident.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outbound connections from the appliance are few and documented
        (serverperch.com for licence check-in and downloads, OS security-advisory
        feeds, and whatever you configure for SMTP, webhooks or directory service). An
        optional allow-list mode is available for regulated environments, and
        fully air-gapped installs use offline licence files and update bundles.
      </p>

      <h2>Agent connections</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Agents connect to your appliance over <strong>mutually authenticated
          TLS&nbsp;1.3</strong> — each machine holds a certificate issued by the
          appliance's own CA, and the appliance identity is pinned at enrolment.
        </li>
        <li>
          Jobs dispatched to agents are <strong>signed</strong>; agents verify the
          signature before acting.
        </li>
        <li>
          Agent self-upgrades verify Ed25519-signed release manifests before
          installing.
        </li>
        <li>
          Machine state lives in your appliance's database with tenant isolation —
          nothing is streamed to ServerPerch.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Licensing</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Licences are Ed25519-signed documents verified against public keys compiled
          into the product — no phone-home is required to validate them.
        </li>
        <li>
          Connected appliances renew a short check-in lease daily; air-gapped
          appliances run from offline licence files.
        </li>
        <li>
          Enforcement is deliberately soft: licence problems degrade purchase and
          download workflows, not your running estate.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Secrets and support data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Appliance-held secrets (CA keys, install credentials, API tokens) are sealed
          under a master key and redacted from UI, API and logs.
        </li>
        <li>
          Support bundles are <strong>pseudonymised by default</strong>: hostnames,
          tenant names, emails, IPs and DNS names become stable HMAC-derived tokens,
          and the map that reverses them stays on your appliance.
        </li>
        <li>
          Bundles are encrypted to the ServerPerch support public key before upload;
          the decryption key is held by support staff, not on our servers. Bundles are
          deleted automatically after 30 days, and every staff access is audit-logged
          and visible to you.
        </li>
        <li>
          Known-sensitive material is never included in a bundle — private keys,
          password hashes, session tokens and TOTP secrets are excluded, and a leak
          test in the release pipeline fails the build if a canary secret appears.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Customer portal accounts</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Passwords hashed with Argon2id; TOTP required for organisation owners.</li>
        <li>Per-user and per-IP rate limiting on sign-in, plus bot checks on sign-up.</li>
        <li>All customer and staff mutations are audit-logged.</li>
        <li>Payments are handled entirely by Stripe — card data never touches us.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.security}`}>{CONTACT.security}</a>. Please
        include reproduction details and allow us reasonable time to investigate
        before public disclosure.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
